may be a direct effect of heat exposure on the respiratory sys-
tem. Studies in asthma suggest that breathing hot humid air may
result in bronchoconstriction that is mediated via cholinergic
pathways(91).
The anticipated effects of climate change also include
increases in temperature variability and in extreme cold weather
conditions. Excessive cold temperatures have been linked to
increases in mortality and morbidity among those with COPD.
In a study of individuals over 65 years in Michigan, those with
COPD had a 19% increased risk of dying on cold days(92).In
a New Zealand study, the death rate was 18% higher in win-
ter versus non-winter months and 31% of excess deaths were
attributable to respiratory disease(93). Studies have also demon-
strated an impact of cold temperatures on lung function and the
