3
  in  
PM
2.5
 concentration.
(51)
 In line with this, a systematic 
review and meta-analysis using six European cohorts 
and involving 16,000 children showed an up to 30% 
increase in NO
2
 exposure-related risk of pneumonia.
(35)
Current studies suggest a possible contributing 
effect of air pollution on the spread of SARS-CoV-2 
(COVID-19). A study conducted in Italy revealed that, 
in  cities  where  the  concentrations  of  air  pollutants  
were  higher  before  the  epidemic,  there  was  an  
accelerated spread of the virus, as well as a higher 
number of infected individuals, when compared with 
less polluted cities. 
(52,53)
