,-0.028369702,-0.035324346,-0.018226419,0.020522762,-0.0085030375,-0.02302906,-0.027949799,0.01969608,-0.013082605,-0.006492095,-0.014276705,0.016258122,-0.012747995,0.016796123,-0.04020572,0.014289826,-0.009552795,-0.0100251855,-0.004176067,0.041911576,0.01598256,-0.0018764419,-0.02497111,-0.028369702,-0.006570827,-0.031702682,-0.02941946,0.0055998014,0.02976063,-0.0071252305,0.004553324,-0.011199603,0.047816463,-0.003487164,-0.014657242,-0.008627696,-0.010766577]'), ('2001a89b-ad4a-49e8-8459-61469b2a844f', 'major studies on the respiratory impact of urban air 
pollution conducted in Brazil.
Air pollution from biomass burning
Over the past 20 years, studies conducted in Brazil 
have assessed the impacts that forest fires (especially 
in the Brazilian Amazon) and pre-harvest sugarcane 
burning (especially in the state of São Paulo) have 
on the health of the exposed population (Chart 2).
Studies  conducted  in  urban  areas  located  in  
sugarcane  producing  regions  in  the  state  of  São  
Paulo have shown that, during the sugarcane burning 
season,  there  were  increases  in  emergency  room  
visits for inhalation therapy
(87)
 and for pneumonia,
(88)
 
as well as an increase in hospitalizations of children 
and elderly individuals for all respiratory diseases,
(89)
 
specifically for asthma.
(90)
 In Monte Aprazível, a town 
in the state of São Paulo, rhinitis prevalence increased 
and lung function decreased in children during the 
sugarcane burning season.
