In this work, the short-term public health effect of air pollution
is assessed in terms of hospital admissions due to respiratory
diseases, the most studied outcome in Brazilian epidemiological
time series studies. Gouveia et al. (2017) studying the largest
Brazilian metropolis (metropolitan area of São Paulo - MASP)
concluded that cardiovascular diseases were statistically relevant
in fewer cities than respiratory diseases, which may be linked to
age range, covariables, and the statistic technique regarding the
regression. As cardiovascular disease is relevant in cities with air
pollution, more studies are necessary to investigate their
relationship with air pollutants. Gouveia et al. (2019) showed
