all ages. Belo Horizonte presented the highest incidence rate of
hospital admissions due to respiratory diseases in children and
the elderly (26.9 in March/2015 and 25.2 in April /2015, per
10,000 inhabitants, respectively), followed by São Paulo. Rio
de Janeiro had the lowest incidence rates for all years, in both
age groups. Differences between the rates in each city are more
pronounced when analyzing the elderly group. After March, the
incidence rate increases, which may be related with the beginning
of the dry season (April to September).
To estimate the avoidable admissions for all ages, the inci-
dence rates were also calculated, but considering the total popu-
lation and the total number of hospital admissions due to
respiratory diseases. It can be noticed that the same pattern
among cities remains, with Belo Horizonte presenting the highest
incidence rates, followed by SãoPaulo, Vitória and, finally, Rio', '{"loc":{"lines":{"to":1352,"from":1337}},"pdf":{"info":{"Title":"Avoiding hospital admissions for respiratory system diseases by complying to the final Brazilian air quality standard: an estimate for Brazilian southeast capitals","Author":"Matheus Augusto Oliveira Fernandes","Creator":"Arbortext Advanced Print Publisher 9.1.440/W Unicode","ModDate":"D:20200826185443+08''00''","Subject":"Environ Sci Pollut Res, doi:10.1007/s11356-020-07772-x","Keywords":"Health benefits,Excess hospitalizations,Diseases of the respiratory system,Relative risks,Economic gains,Brazil","Producer":"Acrobat Distiller 9.0.0 (Windows)","CreationDate":"D:20200129104632+08''00''","IsXFAPresent":false,"PDFFormatVersion":"1.4","IsAcroFormPresent":false},"version":"1.10.100","metadata":{"_metadata":{"dc:title":"Avoiding hospital admissions for respiratory system diseases by complying to the final Brazilian air quality standard: an estimate for Brazilian southeast capitals","dc:format":"application/pdf","dc:creator":"Matheus Augusto Oliveira FernandesWillian Lemker AndreãoFelipe Marinho MacielTaciana Toledo Almeida Albuquerque","dc:subject":"Health benefitsExcess hospitalizationsDiseases of the respiratory systemRelative risksEconomic gainsBrazil","pdfaid:part":"2","dc:publisher":"Environmental Science and Pollution Research","pdf:keywords":"Health benefits,Excess hospitalizations,Diseases of the respiratory system,Relative risks,Economic gains,Brazil","pdf:producer":"Acrobat Distiller 9.0.0 (Windows)","dc:identifier":"10.1007/s11356-020-07772-x","sn:authorinfo":"Taciana Toledo Almeida Albuquerquehttp://orcid.org/0000-0002-6611-0283","xmpmm:history":"converteduuid:710bcb76-b5f3-4681-a01b-a7ca920b7ffbconverted to PDF/A-2bpdfToolbox2020-01-29T10:47:12+08:00converteduuid:ff732b79-377b-426f-9caf-9dc656e5c3c2converted to PDF/A-2bpdfToolbox2020-08-26T18:54:43+08:00","dc:description":"Environ Sci Pollut Res, doi:10.1007/s11356-020-07772-