to five times higher than typical outdoor concentrations.
135
 
According to WHO, almost three million people or 50% of 
the households worldwide use biomass as the main source 
of energy for cooking, heating, and other household needs, 
such as wood, crop residues, and animal dungs in addition 
to coal. Biofuels have higher emission factors for PM and 
other  pollutants,  especially  during  incomplete  combustion 
at lower temperatures.
136
 The burning of biofuels generates 
indoor  airborne  particles  at  levels  much  higher  than  those 
of cleaner fuels
137
 or outdoor levels,
138
 and well above levels 
in  most  polluted  cities.
139
  Such  particles  also  have  small 
aerodynamic diameters (eg, ranging from 0.05 μm to 1 μm 
for wood smoke)
140
 and can penetrate deep into the alveolar 
