accounted for 4148 avoidable hospitalizations, which was associated to $1.1 million public health gains. Results considering
the day of exposure (lag 0) were superior to those with the 5-day moving average (lag 5). The results highlighted the importance
of adopting more restrictive standards and called for public policies, the necessity of expanding the air quality monitoring
network, mapping emission sources, and improve the knowledge about the interaction between air pollution and health outcomes
beyond respiratory disease for the region.
