6692521,-0.0025954433,-0.0033826397,-0.0184316]'), ('9355ca7f-232e-4114-9c25-8982f540687b', 'There has been increased attention to the effects of heat expo-
sure with the anticipated increases in temperature projected in
the context of climate change (12–15).Studiesofheatexpo-
sure are often conducted in the context of heat waves, sustained
periods of extreme heat that occur over consecutive days. Heat
waves can have startling health consequences, as was seen dur-
ing the summer of 2003 when over 70,000 deaths in Europe
were attributable to extreme heat(80)and during the summer
of 1995 when there were approximately 750 heat-related deaths
in Chicago during only 5 days(81). Studies have consistently
found that elderly individuals and those with underlying cardiac
and respiratory diseases, including COPD, are at increased risk
for adverse health effects of heat exposure (82–86). For exam-
ple, a study across 12 U.S. cities estimated that the effect of hot
temperatures during summertime can increase the risk of death
