 WHO published its air quality guidelines in 1987 
and revised them in 1997. Based on the research develop-
ments  thereafter,  they  updated  the  guidelines  for  PM,  O
3
, 
NO
2
, and SO
2
 in 2005.
166
 The values in the WHO guidelines 
are much lower than in the US NAAQS.
167,168
 We focused this 
part of the review on studies conducted in the last 10 years 
to specifically evaluate if the current air quality guidelines 
are protective of COPD sufferers.
A prospective cohort study
119
 in Copenhagen, Denmark, 
with 57,053 participants assessed the effect of exposure to 
traffic air pollution (NO
2
 and nitrogen oxides [NO
x
]) over 
35 years on the incidence of COPD. The modeled 35-year 
mean  of  outdoor  NO
2 
level  was  17.0 μg/m
3
  or  9  ppb  for 
the total population and 18.1 μg/m
3
 or 9.6 ppb for COPD 
patients. These levels were well below the current NAAQS 
