among different races, sexes, and age groups. The rate for 
chronic bronchitis in 2011 (NCHS)
3
 was much higher in non-
Hispanic whites (4.7%) and blacks (4.9%) than in Hispanics 
(2.9%) and other non-Hispanics (2.4%). The rate was twice 
as  high  in  females  (5.7%)  as  in  males  (3.0%).  Prevalence 
rates were the highest among those 65 years or older (6.4%) 
and the lowest among those 18–44 years (2.9%) with 70% of 
cases occurring in those older than 45 years. For emphysema, 
the prevalence rate in 2011 followed a similar pattern among 
ethnic groups, which was the highest for non-Hispanic whites 
(2.4%)  followed  by  blacks  (1.8%),  other  non-Hispanics 
(1.3%), and Hispanics (0.7%). Females surpassed males in 
the  prevalence  rate  (2.1%  vs  1.9%),  although  historically, 
