Air quality guidelines impact on COPD sufferers
functions).  However,  relatively  fewer  of  such  studies  have 
been conducted, and the results on the effects are inconsistent. 
Table 5 presents the details for ten panel studies.
124–133
 One of 
the earliest panel studies was conducted by Lawther et al.
124
 
They used diary cards to assess the symptoms of bronchitis 
related  to  the  change  in  air  pollution  levels  in  patients  and 
found a 28% overall increase in worsened symptom rates. The 
panel studies in the past 25 years showed a variety of exacerba-
tions on the COPD patients ranging from increased respiratory  
symptoms,  blood  pressure,  and  inhaler  and  nebulizer  use, 
reduced lung function, and limits in physical activities to death, 
