140–142
 Since the 
early 1980s, there have been quite a few studies conducted 
to  evaluate  indoor  exposure  to  biomass  air  pollution  and 
the odds of increased chronic bronchitis or/and COPD, par-
ticularly  in  low-income  countries  where  only  lower  grade 
energy resources are available and affordable. Table 6 sum-
marizes 21 studies
143–163
 conducted in low-income countries 
identified through this search in which exposed populations 
were  largely  nonsmoking  females,  but  exposed  to  smoke 
from  cooking  and  heating  using  biomass  fuels  since  early 
childhood with their mothers or later as housewives. Most 
studies 1) were population-based cross-sectional surveys
